#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530652 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left((x-y)\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5\\left(x-y\\right)$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5\\left({\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =5\\left(-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530671 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(2)^2+(1)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x + y))\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{6} (- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530672 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2 x z^{2} + 2 y z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-2\\le x\\le -1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})^{2} + 2 y (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530674 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- x z + y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}) + y (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(4 x^{4} y - 8 x^{3} y^{2} + 2 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} + 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (4 x^{4} y - 8 x^{3} y^{2} + 2 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} + 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530682 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-2\\le x\\le -1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=4 x + y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(4 x + y)^2+(x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(- 4 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530695 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2 y z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 y (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530699 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-2\\le x\\le 0$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530714 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530725 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x z - y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=4 x + y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(4 x + y)^2+(x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1} (- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530730 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=x z - y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530731 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=x z - y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530732 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530747 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530752 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+4- \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530755 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- 3 y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- 3 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530765 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530767 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{3 y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle -2,-2\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle -1,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)-f(-2,-2) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}-6-6 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530769 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530786 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-4,-3,-4)$, $B(-9,1,-10)$, $C(-5,-5,-11).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-9i+j-10k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-5i+4j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-5,4,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-5j-11k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-2))i-((-5)\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-1))j+((-5)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-40i-29j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-40,-29,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-40)^{2}+(-29)^{2}+{14}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2637} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{293}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{293}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530789 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,4,-2)$, $B(0,2,-2)$, $C(-1,2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =i-2j \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j-3k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =2i+j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530792 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(4,-2,3)$, $B(5,-7,-3)$, $C(8,-5,9).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5i-7j-3k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =i-5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨1,-5,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(8i-5j+9k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =4i-3j+6k \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot 4)j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-48i-30j+17k \\\\  & =⟨-48,-30,17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-48)^{2}+(-30)^{2}+{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3493}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{3493}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530811 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,-4,1)$, $B(-1,-5,1)$, $C(0,-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-5j+k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+3k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530812 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(3,-2,4)$, $B(10,0,0)$, $C(6,-7,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(10i)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =7i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨7,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(6i-7j+6k)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 7 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-5))i-(7\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 3)j+(7\\cdot (-5)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-16i-26j-41k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-26,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-16)^{2}+(-26)^{2}+(-41)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2613}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2613}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530819 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-2,-2,-3)$, $B(-1,-2,-1)$, $C(-2,-4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-4j-2k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =4i-j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530826 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,0,1)$, $B(3,-1,-2)$, $C(-1,-2,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i-j-2k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-6k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =-3i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =i+16j-5k \\\\  & =⟨1,16,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{16}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{282}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{282}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530839 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,2,-2)$, $B(3,2,-2)$, $C(2,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j-2k)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-4j \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530844 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,-2)$, $B(0,0,-2)$, $C(2,3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2k)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530848 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-3,-3,-4)$, $B(-6,1,-6)$, $C(-5,-2,-10).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-6i+j-6k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-3i+4j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-2j-10k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-((-3)\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-22i-14j+5k \\\\  & =⟨-22,-14,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-22)^{2}+(-14)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{705}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{705}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531565 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r={3}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531606 :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec0b88ec00ef7e7061af0b08761eb73f05a5f669208babacbd4eb86736019230.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=6{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=18{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(18{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{36{e}^{6\\theta }+324{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{360{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{[{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531626 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531628 :question/problem "![](images/83/83813bff1ff86036e2773d9476150de619688daec9cae2623a555a43290cace3.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531644 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1bf9d989f1fbaeda90325b924f2c9285cd4e37e1948ba63256258b0a84c7e44.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={2}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531731 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+2\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(-4)}-5(3)^2(-4)+2\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{15}{4}\\right)+(180)+(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{713}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531741 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,-8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{2x}y - 2x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,-3,-8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,-3) & =\\frac{2(2)}{(-3)}-2(2)^2(-3) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+(24) \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531747 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,-6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{3x}y - 3x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,-6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{3(3)}{(-4)}-3(3)^2(-4)+3\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{9}{4}\\right)+(108)+(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{435}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531767 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{5(2)}{(3)}-5(2)^2(3) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)+(-60) \\\\  & =- \\frac{170}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531778 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,7)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{2x}y - 2x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,7)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{2(2)}{(3)}-2(2)^2(3)+3\\cos(\\pi (3)) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+(-24)+(-3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{77}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531780 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,-4)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,-4)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{5(2)}{(3)}-5(2)^2(3)+3\\cos(\\pi (3)) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)+(-60)+(-3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{179}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531781 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,0)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{3x}y - 3x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{3(3)}{(-4)}-3(3)^2(-4) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{9}{4}\\right)+(108) \\\\  & =\\frac{423}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
